response:
 Here we have $n=2$ and the matrix is 
\[\mathbf{A}=\begin{pmatrix}  -1 & 2 \\  3 i & 4 \end{pmatrix}.\] The entry in the bottom left position is not an integer, so we will have to rationalize the denominator.  Hence,
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^2&=\begin{pmatrix}  7 & -2  \\  6 &  13 \end{pmatrix} \\
\mathbf{A}^4&=\mathbf{A}^2\cdot\mathbf{A}^2=\begin{pmatrix}  85 & -18  \\  30 &  181 \end{pmatrix} \\
\mathbf{A}^6&=\mathbf{A}^2\cdot\mathbf{A}^4=\begin{pmatrix}  -259 &   6  \\  90 & 2530 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*} 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^3&=\mathbf{A}\cdot\mathbf{A}^2=\begin{pmatrix}  -7 & 14  \\  81 & 124 \end{pmatrix} \\
-3\mathbf{A}&=\begin{pmatrix}  3 & -6  \\  -9 & -12 \end{pmatrix} 
\end{align*}

Consider finding a linear combination of $-\mathbf{A}$ and $\mathbf{A}^2$ that is as close as possible to minimizing the $F_1$ entry of $-\mathbf{A}$.  There are many possible coefficients that are integers.  The $2$ in $-14$ with the $14$ in $14$ gives a difference of $28$ in the first entry.  But $3$ in $316$ and $9$ in $165$ gives a difference of $871$.  With trial and error we determine that the linear combination
\[\mathbf{A}^4+3(-3)\mathbf{A}^2-2\mathbf{A}-83\mathbf{I}\]
gives a perfect zero.  Then
\[p\mathbf{A}+q\mathbf{I}=(\mathbf{A}^0+3(-3)\mathbf{A}^1-2\mathbf{A}^0-83\mathbf{I})\cdot \mathbf{A}^6=(\mathbf{A}^4-9\mathbf{A}^2-2\mathbf{A}-83\mathbf{I})\cdot\mathbf{A}^6=\mathbf{A}^0+3(-3)\mathbf{A}^1+(-9)\mathbf{A}^2-2\mathbf{A}-83\mathbf{I}=A^6+3\mathbf{A}^1-2\mathbf{A}-83\mathbf{I}=A^7-86\mathbf{A}-83\mathbf{I}\]
This means the answer is $\boxed{(1,-86)}$. 
</answer>

